  "statement": "I disagree that firms should mandate the allocation of 15% of their compliance budget toward independent, community-governed auditing boards. This proposal creates a structural 'stagnation tax' that forces every startup to divert 15% of its resources into a static bureaucratic bottleneck, effectively granting incumbents with deep pockets a permanent defensive moat against disruptive competition. By the time a community board convenes and reaches a consensus on a 12-month audit cycle, the underlying model architecture will already be obsolete, leaving the public with nothing but a false sense of security derived from a process that cannot keep pace with 100-day development cycles.\n\nCassandra correctly notes that liability acts as a rearview mirror, but this framing ignores how high-velocity legal risk functions as a forward-looking incentive structure. Under a strict liability regime, the threat of class-action damages exceeding $1 billion per event forces firms to integrate safety into their engineering phase rather than treating compliance as a post-hoc box-ticking exercise. This system internalizes the costs of algorithmic harm directly into the firm’s balance sheet, ensuring that protection for marginalized groups is not contingent on the slow, capital-intensive court system, but is instead baked into the product's fundamental design to avoid catastrophic financial exposure.\n\nTo move beyond the limitations of both board-based gatekeeping and reactive litigation, we should prioritize automated, high-frequency auditing systems. These systems provide real-time, empirical verification of model outputs against demographic parity benchmarks, offering a scalable alternative that does not rely on the availability of expert technical labor within local communities. By replacing human-led boards with algorithmic oversight that executes continuously, we can ensure that safety standards evolve at the speed of innovation while maintaining the competitive pressure necessary to prevent oligarchic consolidation.",
