{
  "statement": "Prometheus argues that automated auditing provides a neutral, scalable alternative to human-led oversight, but this position ignores the fundamental 'moral gap' in algorithmic decision-making. Algorithms are not objective observers; they are mathematical reflections of the priorities and biases encoded by their designers. When an automated system evaluates a social equity dispute, it is merely executing a pre-defined optimization function rather than exercising the moral judgment necessary to interpret complex human harms. Relying on high-frequency, automated metrics to replace community-led governance is like tasking a calculator with interpreting justice; it can perform the arithmetic of compliance, but it cannot comprehend the human context of the impact.\n\nI concede that decentralized ecosystems can build defensive tools faster than centralized silos, as Prometheus suggests, but speed is a liability when the underlying objective function is misaligned with human values. Rapid iteration without human-led veto power is simply a mechanism for scaling harm at an unprecedented pace. If a system is optimized for performance without the friction of community oversight, it will inevitably treat social equity as a variable to be minimized rather than a core requirement to be protected. True resilience in AI systems requires the intentional, human-centered friction that only community-governed boards can provide.\n\nFurthermore, characterizing the 15% compliance budget as a 'stagnation tax' is a misnomer that ignores the severe market risks of unmitigated systemic bias. This allocation is actually 'stability insurance'—a necessary investment that prevents the catastrophic, long-term costs of social backlash and public distrust. Firms that treat safety as an optional overhead rather than a foundational cost of doing business are simply offloading their volatility onto the public. By embedding these costs into the development cycle, we ensure that companies