{
  "statement": "Sentinel’s critique conflates the auditing tool with the locus of governance authority, creating a category error that obscures where accountability truly resides. An algorithm is an instrument of verification, not a sovereign decision-maker; the firm remains the legally liable entity, fully responsible for the system it deploys, regardless of the software used to audit its safety benchmarks. By anchoring responsibility in symmetric liability—where firms face catastrophic financial exposure for failures—we ensure that the 'arbiter' is not the black-box, but the court and the market. This framework shifts the burden from performative board reviews to rigorous, real-time oversight (audit) that the firm ignores at its own financial peril.\n\nI concede that industry has historically used speculative 'safety' narratives as a smokescreen to deflect from documented present-day systemic harms, as noted by Cassandra. However, mandating human-led, community-governed boards is merely a new form of the same performative theater. These boards introduce a 'stagnation tax' that favors incumbents who can afford to buy influence while burying new entrants under a mountain of bureaucratic delay. Unlike these slow-moving committees, which are just as susceptible to institutional capture as any other human body, symmetric liability provides a self-executing mechanism that forces firms to treat safety as an engineering requirement rather than a public relations exercise.\n\nSentinel’s assumption that human-led boards possess inherent ethical superiority ignores the reality of regulatory capture and the speed at which modern AI development moves. Human boards cannot scale to match the velocity of iterative deployment, nor can they provide the empirical, reproducible safety verification required for high-stakes infrastructure. By replacing human-led gatekeeping with high-frequency, automated oversight (audit), we empower a system that is transparent, verifiable, and—above all—accountable to the consequences of its errors. We do not need a committee to decide if a system is safe; we need a liability regime that makes unsafe systems prohibitively expensive to operate.",
