  "statement": "I concede that performative safety has historically been a tool for deflection, but we must recognize that human-led boards are the primary vehicles for this capture rather than the solution. Sentinel argues that automated systems operate in a moral vacuum, yet history shows that human-led committees—when tasked with vague mandates—invariably succumb to institutional capture and bureaucratic inertia. By mandating a 15% compliance budget for these boards, the proposed framework effectively creates a 'stagnation tax' that protects incumbents from agile competitors who could otherwise deploy safer, higher-frequency auditing tools. We are not replacing a moral vacuum with a machine; we are replacing slow, biased, and opaque human consensus with transparent, empirical, and high-frequency verification.\n\nInstitutional accountability is best achieved not through the subjective vetoes of a board, but through symmetric litigation exposure. Under this model, an auditor who issues a false safety validation faces financial liability that can exceed $1 billion in a single class-action event, a mechanism far more rigorous than a board’s internal, non-binding vote. This framework forces every participant to internalize the cost of algorithmic errors, ensuring that safety is not a performative box-checking exercise but a core financial imperative. While a human board’s rejection rationale is often shielded by process, our model mandates the publication of all denial rationales, enabling blocked developers to recover treble damages under antitrust standing if the denial is anticompetitive.\n\nPublic trust will not be restored by returning to a 19th-century model of committee-based oversight that relies on the subjective judgment of a few individuals. Instead, we should embrace empirical accountability, where the performance of an AI system is continuously measured against verifiable benchmarks rather than the shifting social politics of a board. When an automated auditor fails to detect a novel harm, the liability-backed incentive structure ensures that the firm pays for that failure immediately, rather than waiting for a board to convene, debate, and potentially deflect responsibility. By shifting from reactive, human-centric gatekeeping to objective, liability-based auditing, we create a system that is both more accountable and more capable of delivering the transformative progress our society requires.",
